  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "Chef has NNN oranges with him. Each orange has either 10,11,10, 11, 10,11, or 121212 slices.\n\nChef wanted to eat **exactly** KKK slices of orange. However, the oranges were too tasty, and Chef ended up eating all NNN of them!\n\nChef wasn't paying attention, so he didn't count the number of slices he ate, and he also doesn't know how many slices each orange had - the only thing he knows is that each orange has either 10,11,10, 11, 10,11, or 121212 slices.\n\nIs it possible that Chef ate exactly KKK slices from NNN oranges?\n\n\nInput Format\n\nThe first line of input will contain a single integer TTT, denoting the number of test cases.\nEach test case consists of a single line of input, containing two space-separated integers NNN and KKK — the number of oranges, and the number of slices Chef wants to eat.\n\n\nOutput Format\nFor each test case, output on a new line the answer: `\"YES\"` if Chef could have eaten exactly NNN orange slices, and `\"NO\"` otherwise. The output should be without quotes.\n\n\nEach character of the output may be printed in either uppercase or lowercase, i.e, the strings `No`, `NO`, `nO`, and `no` will all be treated as equivalent.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤50001 \\leq T \\leq 50001≤T≤5000",
      "1≤N≤301 \\leq N \\leq 301≤N≤30",
      "1≤K≤5001 \\leq K \\leq 5001≤K≤500"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "",
    "outputFormat": "",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "5\n1 10\n1 9\n2 21\n4 48\n5 49",
        "output": "YES\nNO\nYES\nYES\nNO",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nTest case 111: Chef ate only one orange. It's possible it had 101010 slices, so the answer is \"YES\".\nTest case 222: Chef ate only one orange. It must've had either 10,11,10, 11, 10,11, or 121212 slices, so there's no chance that Chef ate exactly 999 slices.\nTest case 333: Chef ate two oranges. It's possible that one of them had 101010 slices and the other had 111111 slices, for a total of 10+11=2110+11=2110+11=21.\nTest case 444: Chef ate four oranges. If all four had 121212 slices, Chef would've eaten 484848 slices.\nTest case 555: Chef ate five oranges. It can be shown that there's no way for Chef to have eaten five oranges and yet have eaten exactly 494949 slices."
      }
    ]
  },
